  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nSeveral rounds of rain and storms are expected as the pattern \nbecomes more active over the next week. After a long dry period, our \nfirst chance of rain in weeks later tomorrow into the overnight \nperiod. Generally no hazards are expected with this activity, but \ncannot rule out a low chance for localized flash flooding with \nanomalous deep moisture and convergence concentrated east of I-55 \nand south of I-20. Overall most folks should see an inch or less of \nrainfall, but pockets of 2 inches or more in a short period cannot \nbe ruled out. The duration of any heavy rainfall is likely to be \nshort. Given such dry antecedent conditions, any rain would be \nvery beneficial.\n\nShortwave ridging will keep things dry Monday in advance of a cold \nfront. Subtropical jet will aid in lee cyclogenesis off the Rockies \nMonday. The corresponding surface low will rapidly deepen, drawing \nmoisture northward with storm development along its cold front. The \nfront should push through our area mainly overnight Tuesday into \nWednesday morning and progressively weaken as the low lift \nnorthward. Both on WXnext2 and stormnet, show low severe \nprobabilities to our west and into far western portions of our area. \nStormnet lightning probabilities diminish as the system moves east, \nsuggesting weakening by early Wednesday morning. That said, \npotential secondary low development could reinvigorate activity in \nthe southeast Wednesday. As far as severe potential goes, chances \noverall look low. Linear storm mode and high shear low cape type \nenvironment suggest a primary wind/tornado risk, which would likely \nbe isolated with greatest chances west of I-55 and the river. We may \nalso have to watch for a heavy rainfall threat with anomalous PWAT \nconcentrated along the boundary. Some guidance is showing bullseyes \nin the west of 2 or more inches in a short period. More details will \ncome in future forecast updates.\n\nDue to the secondary low development, moisture wrap around lingers, \nso low (15-20%) rain chances continue into Thursday. Additional \nrainfall amounts to generally under a tenth of an inch. The jet \nremains active and persistent moist trajectories may keep isolated \nrain chances around to end the week. /SAS/",
